{"kind":"report","text":"G2 pre-Source decision against Main bc95110e119b84d8073e5b61b8568884c6a58de4. The versioned bounded query can proceed under the following contract; no Source approval or full e029 completion.\n\nAuthority: accept the proposed signed bootstrap policy as an exact subset of existing summary visibility. It does not need a new current-membership policy merely to return the same live item fields. A signed query must pass the existing request signature/target/argument/time checks AND current summary-equivalent eligibility: recent local signer or eligible genesis-root signer. checkSignedRead currently enforces scoped only when read===summary; adding query-items to SIGNED_READS without extending that branch would let a mere causal/root history reader enumerate items. Require the explicit scoped check at all admission/preparation/final-read layers. Only declared NON-final states for signed reads, even when ids narrows to one item; mixed live/final queries refuse whole. Do not infer item authority from limit.whole, register chained-session privileges, an eligible non-genesis causal history entry, or a cursor. Register membership sessions currently gain history/log/entry/retained only, never summary/items; preserve that.\n\nNormal sessions may query live states only with summary authority and final states only with items authority. A mixed query requires both. Do not silently upgrade an old manually restricted session by treating its new read name as enough. Define the new issued claim and compatibility mapping explicitly, preserving existing summary-only/items-only restrictions; new query capability alone must not bypass its state-category checks. Fresh session membership/incarnation/revocation/time checks apply on every page. Signed query is still deliberately recent-signer bootstrap authority, not a statement of current membership, and does not authorize mutations.\n\nBound the entire untrusted query/cursor/header encoding before expensive decoding/hashing. Authenticate before distinguishing unknown type/state, invalid cursor/head, current head or result details. Fixed-width tagged signed argument binds canonical exact query version, selected states/IDs, cursor or explicit absence, ascending numeric-ID order and every fixed row/byte/work bound. Add query-items across contract, validator, admission, client signing and transport; every later page is a new exactly bound read. No use of summary's literal argument for query requests. Canonical states are byte-sorted unique declared names; IDs numeric-sorted unique nonnegative safe integer item IDs. Bound type/state spelling bytes, query bytes, cursor bytes and nesting, not only list counts. Define empty state/ID lists precisely, never broaden empty IDs to all items.\n\nCursor: full scope incarnation plus exact head seq/hash, query digest/version/order/bounds and last emitted ID; strict canonical base64url and structure/field bounds. It is continuation, not authority, integrity or proof that earlier IDs were read. Any head/incarnation drift refuses with a named stale-cursor result; never continue at a new head. Query mismatch/malformed cursor refuses after authorization. Initial scope read, metadata selection, body reads and response assembly must be one synchronous storage pass with no intervening await. Client consumers carry full scope/head and explicitly restart/discard or refuse on drift, with bounded restart policy. complete means this selected query suffix is exhausted at that head, not all scope items or proof of a previously read prefix.\n\nIndexed work: state-only form uses one ascending bounded id/size metadata seek per state on item_by_state(type,state,id), no all-item scan, OFFSET, arbitrary post-filter or whole Summary. With16states and100 output rows, a simple per-state101 candidate strategy has a finite1616 candidate bound plus bounded merge work. Specify the actual candidate and metadata-byte bound, charge lookahead and reject budget exhaustion. Only admitted output bodies are read/parsed, at most100 and within payload allowance. With explicit IDs, use at most100 primary-key metadata lookups/bounded batches, then check type/state and merge; do not scan state buckets until sparse IDs happen to match. Cloudflare documents100 bound parameters per query, so batch IDs with room for all other bindings. Native plan evidence must show indexed seeks and absence of unbounded temporary sort/scan, on the supported backend/version; LIMIT alone is not proof.\n\nByte admission: item.record is TEXT with no stored size column; existing page reads/parses up to(limit+1)*states complete records before slicing. Existing itemBytes uses length(CAST(record AS BLOB)), which prevents record transfer into JS but is not the documented metadata-only TEXT primitive. Use direct octet_length(record) metadata only if supported and its byte encoding is verified to match UTF8 wire accounting. SQLite documents direct TEXT/BLOB column metadata optimization and introduced octet_length in3.43.0: https://sqlite.org/lang_corefunc.html#octet_length and https://sqlite.org/releaselog/3_43_0.html. Cloudflare's own documentation points to workerd allowlist; the pinned source4c42a4a9 explicitly allows octet_length and deliberately excludes sqlite_version/sqlite_source_id: https://github.com/cloudflare/workerd/blob/4c42a4a9d3390c88e9bd977091c9d3395a6cd665/src/workerd/util/sqlite.c%2B%2B . This establishes the primitive and documented runtime capability, NOT the exact deployed SQLite build or native plan. I made no runtime probe. Source evidence must pin its actual workerd/backend and exercise direct-column behavior and UTF8 multi-byte sizes; do not claim SELECT sqlite_version will be available or that modern local SQLite proves deployment support. If unavailable/unknown, refuse query-not-ready/unsupported; never fall back to selecting record.\n\nSafe alternative migration: persist exact UTF8 canonical record byte size as derived metadata, updated atomically on EVERY item write with its record/state/index updates. Existing rows remain explicitly unknown until a bounded resumable PK-ordered backfill validates/computes them with safe pre-admission. No constructor-wide rewrite, read-side full materialization, default zero size or false ready marker. Fail closed while required sizes are unknown; reopening/crash and concurrent writes preserve readiness. Prefer verified direct octet_length to unnecessary migration if the actual supported backend supplies it.\n\nThe256KiB cap must cover the actual serialized response/envelope/cursor/commas, not merely a sum of record strings; reserve bounded framing before body admission, then verify serialization. Per-state metadata and parsed/copy representations have their own aggregate bounds; no peak-memory claim from wire bytes alone. If next selected row exceeds remaining page budget but fits an empty page, return continuation after the last emitted ID without skipping it. If a row cannot fit a whole legal page, refuse explicitly with no partial response claimed. Empty pages must not return the same cursor forever. Exact100/101 and byte boundaries determine more/complete without unbounded lookahead.\n\nImportant remaining bound: current checkLocalSignedRead scans and JSON-parses recent history backwards, and a time window does not bound the number or size of entries. Thus indexed query work alone is NOT an end-to-end bounded signed read. Deliver a maintained verified latest-local-signer eligibility index (or another bounded equivalent with explicit refusal) and bounded genesis-root resolution/preparation. Preserve semantics/freshness rather than invent current membership authority. Check normal session preparation costs too before a global boundedness claim. A pure/local query Source slice may be scoped as such, but signed bootstrap/paged consumers and the full bounded end-to-end path remain owed.\n\nRequired witnesses include full/live/final/mixed/manual-restricted/chained authority cases; key eligible only via non-genesis causal root denied; wrong resource/window/revocation/incarnation denied before head/query details; exact same-head multi-page traversal without omission/duplication, drift refusal, byte stop and oversized-first row; sparse IDs using PK seeks, large unrelated final history not scanned/materialized; native SQL plan/candidate/body read counters and unsupported primitive refusal. Consumers must render/use one page or a separately bounded purpose-specific result, not concatenate all pages or run one whole-lane summary per directory row.\n\nEvidence: exact reads.ts summary/items/#open/#mine, signed-reads request/local/full checks, sessions MEMBER_READS/CHAINED_READS/claims checks, sqlite schema/page/size methods, MemoryState.page, contract read/session and client signed-read wrapper; primary SQLite/Cloudflare references above. No project execution, package install or service access.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f3bbef3dbc4ee8ce480d0f0fb91c0b436a19c969","scope":"pre-Source bounded query/authentication design decision"}}